    <h1>401(k) Limits When Changing Jobs Midyear in 2027</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> The $25,500 employee elective deferral limit ($34,000 at 50+, $37,250 at 60–63) is an <strong>individual per-person limit</strong> across all 401(k) plans in 2027, not a per-employer limit.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Enter Prior-Employer YTD in the Calculator →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example (Under 50, Changing Jobs in July)</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose you are under 50 and contributed <strong>$12,500</strong> at Job A between January and June 2027. You start Job B in July with <strong>12 biweekly paychecks</strong> remaining in 2027:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Remaining 2027 room: $25,500 − $12,500 = <strong>$13,000</strong>.</li>
      <li>Per-paycheck target at Job B: $13,000 ÷ 12 = <strong>$1,083.33</strong> for the first 11 paychecks, and <strong>$1,083.37</strong> on the final paycheck.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Set Your New Paycheck Amount</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Check your final pay stub from your prior employer for 2027 Box 12 (Code D for pre-tax, Code AA for Roth) elective deferrals.</li>
      <li>Open section <strong>C. Changed jobs or starting midyear?</strong> in the calculator and enter that amount in <strong>Prior employer in 2027</strong>.</li>
      <li>Confirm your exact number of remaining 2027 paychecks at your new job. Already over $25,500? Read <a href="/401k-excess-contribution-fix/">how to fix an excess 401(k) contribution before April 15</a>.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

